{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user provided a stitch task (#5142) to implement srd012-yes requirements R2.1-R2.2 (output buffering and performance) and R3.1-R3.2 (exit codes and signal handling) for the `cmd/yes` utility in the go-unix-utils project. The task requires modifying `cmd/yes/main.go` and `cmd/yes/yes_test.go`, extending existing R1.x implementations without rewriting from scratch. Acceptance criteria include: all R2.1-R2.2 and R3.1-R3.2 requirements implemented, differential tests pass against gyes, code compiles, tests pass, and no regressions to R1.x functionality.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Block-write buffering strategy (pre-filling a byte buffer with repeated copies of the output line for bulk writes)\n   - SIGPIPE handling via `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` (project convention for exit 0 on broken pipe)\n   - EPIPE detection using `errors.Is(err, syscall.EPIPE)` as backup to signal handler\n   - Differential testing against GNU reference binary (gyes from Homebrew coreutils)\n   - The distinction between signal-killed processes (GNU yes, exit -1 in Go) vs clean exit (our binary, exit 0)\n   - `pkg/testutils.BuildBinary` and `testutils.RunDiffTests` patterns\n   - Go's bufio.Writer caches errors making post-error Flush() a no-op (key reason for switching to block writes)\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd012-yes.yaml`\n     - Source of truth for requirements R2.1 (buffered output ≥8192 bytes or fixed blocks), R2.2 (flush before exit), R3.1 (exit 0 on SIGPIPE), R3.2 (exit 1 on non-EPIPE errors)\n     - Read to understand exact requirement text\n\n   - `cmd/yes/main.go` (MODIFIED - successfully written)\n     - Core implementation file, extended with R2.x and R3.x functionality\n     - Changed from bufio.Writer to block writes; added EPIPE detection\n     - Final written content:\n     ```go\n     // Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n     // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n\n     package main\n\n     import (\n     \t\"errors\"\n     \t\"os\"\n     \t\"strings\"\n     \t\"syscall\"\n\n     \t\"github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\"\n     )\n\n     const blockSize = 8192\n\n     func main() {\n     \tsys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()\n\n     \tline := buildOutputLine(os.Args[1:])\n     \tblock := fillBlock(line)\n     \tfor {\n     \t\t_, err := os.Stdout.Write(block)\n     \t\tif err != nil {\n     \t\t\tif isEPIPE(err) {\n     \t\t\t\tos.Exit(0)\n     \t\t\t}\n     \t\t\tos.Exit(1)\n     \t\t}\n     \t}\n     }\n\n     func buildOutputLine(args []string) string {\n     \tpositional := stripDashDash(args)\n     \tif len(positional) == 0 {\n     \t\treturn \"y\\n\"\n     \t}\n     \treturn strings.Join(positional, \" \") + \"\\n\"\n     }\n\n     func stripDashDash(args []string) []string {\n     \tif len(args) > 0 && args[0] == \"--\" {\n     \t\treturn args[1:]\n     \t}\n     \treturn args\n     }\n\n     // R2.1: pre-fill a block with repeated copies of the line for bulk writes.\n     func fillBlock(line string) []byte {\n     \tbuf := make([]byte, 0, blockSize+len(line))\n     \tfor len(buf)+len(line) <= blockSize {\n     \t\tbuf = append(buf, line...)\n     \t}\n     \tif len(buf) == 0 {\n     \t\tbuf = append(buf, line...)\n     \t}\n     \treturn buf\n     }\n\n     func isEPIPE(err error) bool {\n     \treturn errors.Is(err, syscall.EPIPE)\n     }\n     ```\n\n   - `cmd/yes/yes_test.go` (NOT YET MODIFIED - still needs updates)\n     - Existing content has TestDiff with 6 output comparison test cases and captureLines helper\n     - Needs: TestExitCodeOnSIGPIPE function, captureExitCode helper, `errors` import added\n     - Current content:\n     ```go\n     // Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n     // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n\n     package main\n\n     import (\n     \t\"bufio\"\n     \t\"bytes\"\n     \t\"context\"\n     \t\"os/exec\"\n     \t\"testing\"\n     \t\"time\"\n\n     \t\"github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/testutils\"\n     )\n\n     func TestDiff(t *testing.T) {\n     \tt.Parallel()\n     \tgoBin := testutils.BuildBinary(t, \".\")\n     \trefBin, err := exec.LookPath(\"gyes\")\n     \tif err != nil {\n     \t\tt.Skip(\"reference binary not found\")\n     \t}\n     \ttests := []struct {\n     \t\tname  string\n     \t\targs  []string\n     \t\tlines int\n     \t}{\n     \t\t{\"default-y\", nil, 5},\n     \t\t{\"single-arg\", []string{\"hello\"}, 5},\n     \t\t{\"multi-args\", []string{\"hello\", \"world\"}, 5},\n     \t\t{\"dash-dash-arg\", []string{\"--\", \"--help\"}, 3},\n     \t\t{\"dash-dash-only\", []string{\"--\"}, 3},\n     \t\t{\"empty-string\", []string{\"\"}, 3},\n     \t}\n     \tfor _, tc := range tests {\n     \t\tt.Run(tc.name, func(t *testing.T) {\n     \t\t\tt.Parallel()\n     \t\t\tgoOut := captureLines(t, goBin, tc.args, tc.lines)\n     \t\t\trefOut := captureLines(t, refBin, tc.args, tc.lines)\n     \t\t\tif !bytes.Equal(goOut, refOut) {\n     \t\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"output mismatch\\ngo:  %q\\nref: %q\", goOut, refOut)\n     \t\t\t}\n     \t\t})\n     \t}\n     }\n\n     func captureLines(t *testing.T, binary string, args []string, n int) []byte {\n     \tt.Helper()\n     \tctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 5*time.Second)\n     \tdefer cancel()\n     \tcmd := exec.CommandContext(ctx, binary, args...)\n     \tstdout, err := cmd.StdoutPipe()\n     \tif err != nil { t.Fatalf(\"StdoutPipe: %v\", err) }\n     \tif err := cmd.Start(); err != nil { t.Fatalf(\"Start: %v\", err) }\n     \tvar buf bytes.Buffer\n     \tscanner := bufio.NewScanner(stdout)\n     \tfor i := 0; i < n && scanner.Scan(); i++ {\n     \t\tbuf.Write(scanner.Bytes())\n     \t\tbuf.WriteByte('\\n')\n     \t}\n     \tstdout.Close()\n     \t_ = cmd.Wait()\n     \treturn buf.Bytes()\n     }\n     ```\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - No runtime errors encountered yet (build and test have not been run)\n   - Design issue identified: bufio.Writer caches errors internally, making Flush() after a write error a no-op. This means R2.2 cannot be properly satisfied with bufio.Writer. Fixed by switching to block writes where each Write() sends the full block, making R2.2 trivially satisfied (no separate buffer to flush).\n   - Design issue identified: GNU yes dies from SIGPIPE signal (ExitCode() returns -1 in Go) while our binary exits cleanly with code 0 per R3.1. This means exit code differential testing against gyes would fail. Fixed by making the exit code test verify Go binary only (exit 0), not compare against reference.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Solved the bufio.Writer flush-after-error problem by switching to block writes\n   - Solved the SIGPIPE handler race condition (signal handler goroutine vs main goroutine) with belt-and-suspenders EPIPE check\n   - Identified that exit code differential testing is inappropriate for SIGPIPE behavior (GNU vs Go conventions differ)\n   - The main.go has been written successfully; test file still needs updating\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - Message 1: The massive stitch task prompt containing the full project context, SRDs, architecture, task description (#5142), requirements (R2.1, R2.2, R3.1, R3.2), design decisions (D1: extend not rewrite, D2: add differential tests), acceptance criteria (AC1-AC5), and all constitutions/style guides.\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Write updated `cmd/yes/yes_test.go` with TestExitCodeOnSIGPIPE and captureExitCode helper\n   - Run `go build -o bin/yes ./cmd/yes/` to verify compilation (AC3)\n   - Run `go test ./cmd/yes/ -count=1` to verify tests pass (AC4)\n   - Verify no regressions to R1.x functionality (AC5)\n\n8. Current Work:\n   I was in the middle of implementing srd012-yes R2.1-R2.2 and R3.1-R3.2. I successfully wrote the updated `cmd/yes/main.go` with block writes, EPIPE detection, and proper exit code handling. I was about to write the updated `cmd/yes/yes_test.go` to add exit code testing, then run build and test verification.\n\n   The planned test file additions are:\n   - Add `\"errors\"` to imports\n   - Add `TestExitCodeOnSIGPIPE` function that builds the Go binary, runs it, closes the pipe, and verifies exit code is 0\n   - Add `captureExitCode` helper that runs a binary, reads one line, closes stdout, and returns the process exit code\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Write the updated `cmd/yes/yes_test.go` file with the new TestExitCodeOnSIGPIPE test and captureExitCode helper, then run `go build -o bin/yes ./cmd/yes/` and `go test ./cmd/yes/ -count=1` to verify compilation and test passage. This directly continues the task: the acceptance criteria require \"Differential tests pass against gyes for the new requirements\" (AC2), \"go build ./cmd/yes/ compiles without errors\" (AC3), and \"go test ./cmd/yes/ -count=1 passes\" (AC4).\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/pdiddy/.claude/projects/-private-var-folders-dl-cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn-T-go-unix-utils-worktrees-5142/efd60324-6f55-4840-bac7-09d47bdedc87.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"efd60324-6f55-4840-bac7-09d47bdedc87","uuid":"27b09ed7-59ba-40f0-bee8-80022f7bf5f1","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:55:04.599Z","isSynthetic":true}
